A||||2||DJRice||trip odometer
Z||000000||DJRice||06-22-99||11:42 AM||||I had a inoperative trip odometer on my 87 SE v6. (i have the 120 mph speedo and extra gauge kit) Naturally I delved right in to fix it, and though I discovered the problem, I ruined the electric motor in the process.  I have checked the 84-85 fieros, and their trip counter motors are different.<br>Does anyone know if pontiac may have used this small electric motor on other 87-88 cars ?  Thanks in advance for the assistance.<br>
Z||000001||Bozone||06-23-99||12:04 PM||||I have a speedo from an 84 in my hand. Is the number HSI 33460 on it and the regular odometer both? If not, what number IS on it and is it the same. The trip odo on my 87 has never worked right and I was thinking of tearing into it too. How did you burn the motor? Looks to me like the whole speedo has to be out of the car to even look at it.
Z||000002||DJRice||06-23-99||03:34 PM||||I did have the whole speedo unit out of the car, and the motor (labeled M1056)is a small motor with a black gear box on it.  The 1984 unit is just a motor with a gear on it.  The 1987 unit uses a 4 wire interface and the 1984 unit uses a 2 wire interface.  <br>I did not burn the motor up.  The problem with the inoperative counters seems to be that the motor shaft (a small pin) looses its ability to turn the gear box (apparently its held by some adhesive or something) and the numbers either do not move at all or wont move past 9/10ths of a mile.  I figured that if I bent the extremely small shaft a bit that it would bind inside the hole in the gear it turns and the problem would be fixed.  <p>I broke the stupid little pin.  I was hoping that someone knew what other pontiac models besides Fiero that that trip odometer unit may have been used it.  I guess I may just have to hit the junkyard and go find an 87 grand am or something to gut.
